WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
SPEAKER 1: Aristotle
writes, this alone

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.538
is denied even to God to make
what has been not to have been.

00:00:06.538 --> 00:00:09.884
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.235
So first of all,
why study history?

00:00:17.235 --> 00:00:22.745
It comes from a Greek word means
inquiry, looking into things.

00:00:22.745 --> 00:00:24.870
The first book of history
was written by Herodotus.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.640
It's called The Histories.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.478
It's a story of the past.

00:00:28.478 --> 00:00:30.270
And of course, not
everything in the past--

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.510
significant things in the past,
things that we can learn from,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.260
things that stand for something.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.420
It's just a piece,
really, of this wish

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.250
that we have to understand.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.790
In his Metaphysics, Aristotle
says in the first line,

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:48.320
the human soul stretches
itself out to know.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.838
We like to know.

00:00:49.838 --> 00:00:51.380
And one of the things
we like to know

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.390
is we like to know
about ourselves

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:56.600
because, unlike other
creatures, we're

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.260
not ruled simply by instinct.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.940
We have to choose our way--

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.920
which way should we go?

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.489
And then the second thing
is we look at others.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:07.430
And we are unusually
well-equipped to do this

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:08.840
because we can talk.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.850
We can explain
things to each other.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.580
We can tell each other about
our innermost thoughts.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:18.320
So history just
expands the scope.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.490
And the past is the only
thing we can study intensely.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.650
The present is fleeting.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.350
Past is fixed, at
least according

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:29.420
to the old school of thought.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.710
These days, it's a
deconstructionist age,

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:38.870
and what we think is we're our
own special time, a development

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:42.230
or a progress on previous times.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:47.510
And that sets up a way to
reinterpret those old times so

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.090
that they fit our categories.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:53.430
And we tend to look
down on them a bit, too.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:57.110
And that seems to
me exactly wrong.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.830
Aristotle writes, this
alone is denied even to god

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:04.250
to make what has been
not to have been.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.970
So history-- it
gives us a fixity

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.850
that is made up of
things that at the time

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:16.080
were constantly shifting
and hard to estimate.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.700
When we're picking
what we're going

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.370
to do from among
various options,

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.970
we always have two
things in mind.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:25.170
One is, what would be
the right thing to do.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:29.160
And the other is, what
is it possible to do?

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.760
And you can't always do
the purely right thing.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.380
Well, if you go back and
study the people in the past,

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.700
they're in the same situation.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.340
And yet, in their case,
the whole story is known.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.620
It's not changing anymore.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.050
And you can go back,
and you can put yourself

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:52.290
in the shoes of
Winston Churchill

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:53.880
or of Abraham Lincoln--

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.980
those, by the way,
are two things

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.760
that are sublime to
do, beautiful to do,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.410
important to do.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:05.950
And you can be with them where
Lincoln faces the question,

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.650
should I let the South go?

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.000
Look at the body count.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.980
Lincoln grappled
with that question.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:13.990
You can grapple with him.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:17.860
Churchill-- should we fight
to the death against Hitler?

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.870
He's offering a deal.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.690
So the point is,
there's a lot to know.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.820
And what can you know?

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.210
Can you know it's
now right or wrong

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:30.520
to do what we're going
to do in some country

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.140
where we've had wars?

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:36.130
No, you can just know
that a serious man

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.890
had caution about that.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.450
And what were the
factors that he

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.160
evaluated to make up his mind?

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.690
You could look at
the same factors--

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.160
they may be different today.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.940
But all of that depends
on treating history

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:53.410
as if it is made by people who,
although very different from us

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:58.060
in many ways, are
yet still people--

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.740
the animals that can
talk to each other,

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:05.330
learn from each other, recognize
good and evil, which are all,

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:06.820
by the way--

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:10.360
we have of course on Aristotle
where we explain this--

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.250
those are all related.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.770
Our ability to see good and
evil and our ability to talk

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:17.029
boil down to the same thing.

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:18.640
And so a creature
like that which

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.450
lives in a perishable
body and has needs--

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:25.420
how does it steer itself
by the ultimate truth

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:29.560
when, in its needs, it's
just like any animal?

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.480
Well, that's the human test.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.990
And the people in the past
had to go through that test.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:38.730
Good history, then,
will be an accurate,

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.580
insofar as it's humanly
possible to make it,

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.520
picture of what
happened in the past,

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.560
with a sympathy
for the people so

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.270
that one can learn from them.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.940
They made mistakes, yeah.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.490
And don't we?

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.160
Do we live in a
perfect world today?

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.890
If we don't, then they were
grappling with the same thing

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.870
we are.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.800
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you for watching.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.770
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlights series

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.680
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:08.850
online courses.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:13.010
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

